 [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "9 – 11 August 1928"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/288"} "Zwemstadion, Amsterdam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/4915"} "12 from 7 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The customary strong American presence was countered in this event by a pair of British swimmers, and 17 year old " [:a {:href "/athletes/49375"} "Zus Braun"] ", considered as the host nation’s best chance of an Olympic championship in the swimming pool. Braun had been the silver medalist behind her compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/922281"} "Willy den Turk"] " at the previous year’s European Championships, but had been steadily improving since then. Den Turk fell ill in the run-up to the Olympics and was advised to miss the Games for medical reasons. If Dutch hopes were dampened by the sight of the Scottish swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/47082"} "Ellen King"] " breaking the world record in the first heat, these worries soon passed when Braun replied by swimming four tenths of a second inside this new time."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final saw Braun, roared on by the home crowd, survive a poor finish to hold off the challenge of King and her fellow Briton " [:a {:href "/athletes/46996"} "Joyce Cooper"] ", to become the first Dutchwoman to win an individual Olympic event. Braun, whose mother was the coach to the Dutch team, attempted to defend her Olympic title " [:a {:href "/results/5010"} "four years later in Los Angeles"] ", but an apparent insect bite developed into a serious case of blood poisoning that kept her out of the final. After spending weeks in a Los Angeles hospital, Braun returned home to Rotterdam and gave a press conference, where she alleged her illness was not caused by an insect but was the result of a deliberate attempt to injure her. Speculation involved a possible link to illegal betting. Another finalist in 1928 was the 14 year old " [:a {:href "/athletes/51060"} "Eleanor Holm"] " who began an international career that would veer between brilliance and controversy over the next decade."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/49375"} "Zus Braun"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "1:21.6 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "1:22.0 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/47082"} "Ellen King"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1:22.0 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "1:22.2 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/46996"} "Joyce Cooper"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1:24.2 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "1:22.8 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51032"} "Marian Gilman"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "1:24.0 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "1:24.2 (4)"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51060"} "Eleanor Holm"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; verti